.TH MKSCRIPT 1 "2026-08-10" "mkscript 1.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mkscript \- create executable Bash script skeletons safely
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkscript
[\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-strict\fR]
[\fB\-g\fR|\fB\-\-global\fR]
[\fB\-l\fR|\fB\-\-link\fR]
[\fB\-c\fR]
[\fB\-r\fR]
[\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.I path
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkscript
creates an executable Bash script at the requested path or manages a global link in the
user's detected bin directory.
By default it writes the standard Bash shebang followed by a small comment header
with the script name, a blank description line, the local creation date, and the
creator login name:
.PP
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Script: hello-world
# Description:
# Created: YYYY-MM-DD
# Creator: login-user
.EE
.PP
When
.B \-s
or
.B \-\-strict
is supplied,
.B mkscript
also writes:
.PP
.EX
set -euo pipefail
.EE
.PP
When
.B \-g
or
.B \-\-global
is supplied,
.B mkscript
also creates a symlink in the detected user bin directory
using the script basename.
.PP
.B \-l
or
.B \-\-link
links an existing local file into that same user bin directory
after confirmation.
.PP
.B \-c
checks whether the expected global symlink already exists and prints its path when it does.
.PP
.B \-r
removes the expected global symlink after confirmation.
.PP
Existing files, symlinks, and directories are never overwritten.
.PP
On Linux,
.B mkscript
uses
.BR ~/.local/bin .
On macOS, it prefers a personal local bin directory already on
.B PATH
before falling back to
.BR ~/.local/bin .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-strict
Add
.B set -euo pipefail
after the shebang.
.TP
.B \-g, \-\-global
Create a symlink in the detected user bin directory
that points to the new script.
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-link
Create only a symlink in the detected user bin directory
for an existing local file.
.TP
.B \-c
Check whether the expected global symlink exists and print its path when it does.
.TP
.B \-r
Remove the expected global symlink after confirmation.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display usage information and exit successfully.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display the program version and exit successfully.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 64
Command-line usage error.
.TP
.B 1
The requested link was not present for check or removal mode.
.TP
.B 73
The target script could not be created safely.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Create a simple script in the current directory:
.EX
mkscript hello-world
.EE
.TP
Create a strict-mode script:
.EX
mkscript --strict deploy.sh
.EE
.TP
Create a strict-mode script with a global shortcut:
.EX
mkscript -g deploy.sh -s
.EE
.TP
Link an existing local file into the global bin directory:
.EX
mkscript test.sh -l
.EE
.TP
Check a global link:
.EX
mkscript test -c
.EE
.TP
Remove a global link:
.EX
mkscript -r test
.EE
